course: dictionary
lesson: 1861
title: Cohesion: making writing hang together
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Cohesion is how well the parts of a piece of writing stick together, so it reads as one whole and not as a pile of separate sentences. Think of it as glue. You can feel when it is missing: each sentence may be fine, but the reader keeps getting lost.
explain: Look at this: «Max missed the bus. He ran all the way to school.» The word he links back to Max, so the reader knows who ran. Pronouns like he, she and it are one way writers create cohesion. Linking words are another: «Max missed the bus. However, he got to school on time.»
explain: Other glue words work the same way. «Later that day» and «Meanwhile» show when things happen, and «Outside» shows where. Repeating a key word helps too, and so do «because» and «as a result», which show how one idea leads to the next.
fact: Cohesion means the parts of a text are linked so that it reads as one whole.
fact: Cohesion is the glue that holds sentences and paragraphs together.
fact: Pronouns like he, she and it link back to a name or thing you have already mentioned.
fact: Linking words such as «however», «then» and «as a result» show how ideas connect.
fact: In science, cohesion is the pull between particles of the same kind, such as water molecules sticking together to form drops.
exercise: «Maya lost her scarf. She searched the whole park.» Which word is the pronoun that links back to Maya? | Whole | *She | Searched | Park
exercise: «The rain stopped. ___, we went outside to play.» Which word fits best to show what happened next? | Blue | Gently | *Then | Under
exercise: «The bridge was closed, ___ we took the long way round.» Which phrase shows that one idea leads to the next? | Happily | Blue | Under | *as a result
test: «Sam bought a kite. ___ it flew high above the field.» Which word links back to the kite? | *It | Lorry | Under | Quickly
test: ___, the sky turned dark and the wind began to howl. Which time opener fits best? | *Later that day | Blue | Furry | Sideways
exam: Which of these best describes cohesion in writing? | *How well the parts of a text link together so it reads as one whole | How loudly a story is read aloud | How many pages a book has | Only the spelling of the words
